    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Recurrence Relation for a Dividing Function"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Mathematically speaking, the master theorem provides a solution to the recurrence relation. Let's learn how we can find the recurrence relation for a dividing function."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have a program like this."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "def test(n):\n    if n > 0:\n        print(n)\n        test(n // 2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Given that the test() function takes T(n) time to execute,"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Recurrence Relation for a Dividing Function",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "From the image, we can say"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "T(n)=T(n2)+1+1T\\lparen n\\rparen = T \\left( \\cfrac{n}{2} \\right) + 1 + 1  T(n)=T(2n​)+1+1T(n)=T(n2)+2\\hphantom{T\\lparen n\\rparen} = T \\left( \\cfrac{n}{2} \\right) + 2T(n)=T(2n​)+2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "We can simply represent the constant 2 with c."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "T(n)=T(n2)+cT\\lparen n\\rparen = T \\left( \\cfrac{n}{2} \\right) + cT(n)=T(2n​)+c"
    },
    {
